# Gradient descent, explained

We minimize a loss $L(\theta)$ by repeatedly stepping **downhill** — against the
gradient — with a learning rate $\eta$:

$$
\theta_{t+1} = \theta_t - \eta \, \nabla L(\theta_t)
$$

The whole loop is three moves: evaluate, differentiate, step.

```{mermaid}
flowchart LR
  init["initialize θ"] --> grad["compute ∇L(θ)"]
  grad --> step["θ ← θ − η∇L(θ)"]
  step -->|"not converged"| grad
  step -->|"converged"| done["return θ"]
```

::: {.callout-tip}
## Choosing the learning rate
Too small and training crawls; too large and the loss diverges. A good first guess
is the largest η that still decreases the loss every step.
:::

# Why this loop feels instant

There is no per-edit startup cost. The server stays warm and so does the kernel,
so changing one line re-renders just the block you touched — scroll position and
live state untouched.

::: {.callout-note}
## Click to source
Double-click any block in the preview and the editor jumps to the exact line it
came from, even across included files.
:::
